.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt BIND 2
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm bind
.Nd assign a local address to a socket
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn bind "int s" "const struct sockaddr *addr" "socklen_t addrlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn bind
system call gives the socket
.Fa s
the local address
.Fa addr ,
of
.Fa addrlen
bytes.
.Pp
For a local socket,
.Fa addr
is a
.Vt struct sockaddr_un .
A path name creates a socket file, an abstract name is claimed in the
abstract namespace, and an unnamed address binds the socket to a fresh
abstract name; see
.Xr unix 4 .
.Pp
For an Internet socket,
.Fa addr
is a
.Vt struct sockaddr_in .
Port 0 picks a free ephemeral port.
The family and the address part are not checked: the system has one
interface address.
A UDP port already bound fails at once; a TCP port is claimed, and a
conflict reported, by
.Xr listen 2 .
A UDP or TCP socket that sends or connects without being bound is bound
to an ephemeral port then.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission is denied on a directory of the path, or write
permission on the directory in which the socket file would be created.
.It Bq Er EADDRINUSE
Something exists at the path, the abstract name or the UDP port is in use,
or no ephemeral port is free.
.It Bq Er EAFNOSUPPORT
The address given for a local socket is not of family
.Dv AF_UNIX .
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa s
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The local or UDP socket is already bound, or the address is too short
(or, for a local socket, longer than a
.Vt struct sockaddr_un ) .
.It Bq Er EISCONN
The TCP socket is already connected or listening.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path is 108 bytes or longer.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A directory of the path does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa s
is not a socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr getsockname 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr unix 4
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Fn bind
system call conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2008 .
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn bind
system call first appeared in
.Bx 4.2 .
